#: ../../plugins.rst:1
msgid "Write a plugin"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:4
msgid "A plugin adds a service to Guidedog. A build plugin can transform source text, transform a rendered HTML page, or supply static files. A publisher sends a finished site to a destination. Both are ordinary Rust traits from the ``gd-plugins`` crate. We implement one once. The same value can be linked into a custom ``guidedog`` binary or served as a separate program."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:10
msgid "This chapter builds a product badge. It replaces ``{{product}}`` in source text, adds a label to HTML pages, and supplies the label's CSS. GitHub Pages then shows how the same plugin foundation supports a service with external effects."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:14
msgid "1. Understand the boundary"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:17
msgid "A build plugin implements ``gd_plugins::BuildPlugin``. Its ``process_source``, ``process_html`` and ``collect_assets`` methods are optional. The default methods mean the service is absent (text passes through, no assets). ``load`` reads the plugin's configuration into the value itself. Dropping the value releases what it holds. The host owns the build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:25
msgid "Source hooks precede parsing. HTML hooks and generated assets enter staging. Publication follows a successful build."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:25
msgid "Build services precede publication."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:52
msgid "Source hooks run before parsing each primary RST or Markdown document. They apply to PDF and text builds too. Includes remain the engine's responsibility. HTML hooks receive complete pages, including generated indexes and additional pages. Generated assets belong to HTML builders and live below ``_static/plugins/ID/``. The host collects them once during plugin setup, before reading documents. They are fixed for that build. Source and HTML hooks cannot add later assets."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:59
msgid "A ``Plugin`` is build hooks or a publisher, never both. Normal builds cannot invoke a publishing service. A plugin reaches a build in one of two ways:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:62
msgid "Linked"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:63
msgid "A custom ``guidedog`` binary compiles the plugin crate in and passes its plugins to the command line with ``gd_cli::run_with``. Rust has no stable ABI, so there is no loading of dynamic libraries. Linked code is trusted like the rest of the binary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:67
msgid "Served"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:68
msgid "The plugin crate's small binary calls ``gd_plugins::serve_plugin``. We register it with ``guidedog plugin register``, which pins its SHA-256. Then it runs as a child process per build, behind a bounded JSON Lines protocol. A crash or stall becomes a diagnostic. Registration is explicit, and it is not a sandbox: an installed plugin is a trusted program."
msgstr ""

