#: ../../plugins.rst:170
msgid "5. Read the implementation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:173
msgid "The complete plugin below is the source compiled in step 2. Download :download:`badge.rs <_static/repository/build-plugin-badge.rs>` and :download:`main.rs <_static/repository/build-plugin-main.rs>` to keep a local copy. In our own crate, ``badge.rs`` becomes the library (``src/lib.rs``) and ``main.rs`` the binary, with ``gd-plugins`` and ``gd-toml`` as dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:182
msgid "``plugin()`` is the public descriptor. ``Plugin::build`` keeps the unloaded ``Badge`` as a prototype and clones it for every session, so sessions never share state. ``info`` names the plugin and declares its hooks. ``load`` reads the configuration through ``LoadContext::read_config``. That call bounds its size, declares the file as a dependency and fingerprints the bytes. ``load`` then checks the setting and keeps it in the value."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:188
msgid "The source hook returns ``Cow::Borrowed`` when the text has no marker, so unchanged text is never copied. It checks the expanded size against ``MAX_PLUGIN_TEXT`` before allocating. It replaces every marker, including markers inside code blocks. This is a text transformation, not a markup parser."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:193
msgid "The HTML hook escapes the label and inserts it before the last closing body tag. It uses ``assets_url`` supplied by the host, so nested pages link to the right CSS. This small example expects literal lowercase closing head and body tags. A plugin that rewrites arbitrary HTML should use a suitable parser and state its contract."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:198
msgid "The assets hook returns ordinary owned values. There is nothing to release. Hook results are plain ``Cow``, ``String`` and ``Vec`` values that the host takes over."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:201
msgid "The program that serves the same plugin over standard streams is three lines:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:206
msgid "``serve_plugin`` handles negotiation and dispatch and returns the exit code. Keep protocol output on stdout, and write human logs to stderr."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:209
msgid "6. Link it into our own guidedog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:212
msgid "A plugin used by every build on a machine, or shipped with a team's tools, can be compiled into a custom ``guidedog`` binary instead of being registered. We create a binary crate that depends on ``gd-cli``, ``gd-plugins`` and our plugin crate (``badge`` here), and give it this ``main``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:236
msgid "The binary accepts every ``guidedog`` command and option. A build looks up each ID in ``plugins`` among the linked plugins first, then in the machine registry. So the project's ``conf.toml`` is the same for both deployments and needs no registration step. ``guidedog plugin list`` shows linked plugins beside registered ones."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:241
msgid "A linked plugin's identity in the cache is its ID, its version and the host binary's own build identity, because its code is part of the host. Rebuilding the binary rebuilds the affected output. Linked code runs in the host process, so a panic or a hang in a hook reaches the build. We serve a plugin as a program when it should be isolated."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:247
msgid "7. Keep state and caching explicit"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:250
msgid "A plugin value *is* one build's session. The host creates a fresh session per selected plugin per build, from the prototype. It drops the session when the build ends, whether it succeeded or failed. ``Drop`` replaces an unload callback: we close files or connections there."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:255
msgid "Use ``LoadContext::read_config`` and ``read_file`` during load. They fingerprint the bytes actually read and declare dependencies. Keep loaded state unchanged throughout the build. If we read other files ourselves, we declare their paths with ``declare_dependency`` and add a fingerprint of the loaded values with ``add_cache_key``. Selected environment values go there too. The context only appends, so the fingerprints the helpers recorded cannot be erased. A plugin must not depend on undeclared changing data."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:263
msgid "The host includes identity, configuration, dependencies, cache key, builder, language and generated assets in cache invalidation. Hooks take ``&mut self``. When reading workers share a session, the host serializes calls through a ``Mutex``, so plugins need no synchronization. Installed programs have a deadline per whole request, including pipe writes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:271
msgid "Guidedog loads a plugin and collects HTML assets before reading sources. Hooks run during reading and rendering. The session is dropped after success or failure."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:271
msgid "Build-plugin lifecycle. Asset collection belongs to setup."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:291
msgid "Text is limited to 8 MiB per hook and configuration to 1 MiB. Each plugin may supply at most 256 assets and 16 MiB of asset bytes, and declare at most 256 dependencies. Paths must be portable and relative. Duplicate names, case-only collisions, traversal and conflicts with project files are refused. ``--untrusted`` refuses any build that selects plugins. A child process's memory is outside the host's build budget."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:297
msgid "8. Explain failures and test them"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:300
msgid "Return a ``Problem`` with a code, title, affected path, explanation, concrete correction and nonzero exit status: ``Problem::new(code, title, message)`` with ``.hint(...)``, ``.path(...)`` and ``.exit(...)``. For example, ``product = 42`` is invalid. The badge explains that a string is required and suggests ``product = \"Orbit\"``. The host adds which plugin stopped and in which stage. Markup diagnostics after expansion identify plugin-expanded input and show its actual excerpt. They do not pretend expanded line numbers belong to the original."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:308
msgid "Test the plugin value directly with ``PluginSession::load`` and its hook methods, and through its program with ``process_plugin``. Cover changed settings, unchanged rebuilds, nested links, invalid markup, asset collisions, cancellation and timeouts. A failed build must preserve the previous site. The crate's own tests run the badge both linked and served and require identical outputs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:314
msgid "9. Add a publishing service"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:317
msgid "A publisher implements ``gd_plugins::Publisher`` and is wrapped with ``Plugin::publisher``. Its methods are ``info``, ``load``, ``plan``, ``publish``, and optional ``status`` and ``recover``. A session holds configuration and credentials. A ``Snapshot`` freezes the checked site files. A ``Plan`` compares that snapshot with a target baseline. An ``Outcome`` reports the verified result. These are ordinary values, and there is nothing to release."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:324
msgid "Start with :download:`publisher.rs <_static/repository/publisher.rs>` (``crates/gd-plugins/examples/publisher.rs``), a working publisher that replaces a local folder whole. Then follow the GitHub Pages publisher in ``crates/gd-pages``. Configuration and machine binding establish authority. Planning reads the target. Publishing writes a durable journal before one conditional Git push. Status and recovery settle the outcome. Nothing in a source or HTML hook grants publication authority. ``guidedog publish --publisher=ID --config=FILE`` builds the project and runs a publisher. ``--dry-run`` stops after the plan."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:333
msgid "A remote acceptance is not a live deployment. ``Accepted`` and ``Pending`` return 6. An uncertain write returns ``Unknown`` and 7, with status or recovery directions. Never retry an uncertain write blindly. See :doc:`howto/github-pages` for the complete user workflow."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:338
msgid "10. Add a command to guidedog"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:341
msgid "A plugin can also add commands to guidedog's own command line. The badge adds one:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:348
msgid "The GitHub Pages plugin works the same way: ``guidedog publish pages`` and ``guidedog pages workflow`` are its commands, not guidedog's. We declare a command with ``commands`` and run it with ``run_command``:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:366
msgid "The words of the name decide where the command appears. ``badge preview`` is a new command; ``publish NAME`` is a target of ``guidedog publish``. A plugin cannot replace a command guidedog has. The summary, help and options we declare become the command's ``--help`` and its line under PLUGIN COMMANDS in ``guidedog --help``, and a mistyped name gets a \"Did you mean\" that knows our command."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:368
msgid "The ``CommandContext`` is everything guidedog lends the command. ``print`` and ``eprint`` write to its output. ``ask`` and ``confirm`` put a question to the person, and answer nothing when nobody can be asked (a script, a CI job), so a command never hangs. ``build_project`` has guidedog build a project, HTML or PDF, into a folder we name, with its engine and its linked plugins. ``dir`` is the folder the command was started in, and ``config`` the plugin's configuration file when the project's ``plugin_configs`` names one. We return the exit status, or a ``Problem`` with a hint, which guidedog reports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:370
msgid "The same command works in both deployments. Linked, it runs inside guidedog. Registered, guidedog starts our program with ``--guidedog-command`` and a request, on the same terminal, so questions and progress reach the person directly; ``serve_plugin`` answers that request without any code of ours. ``guidedog plugin inspect ID`` lists the commands a plugin adds."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:372
msgid "11. Version and distribute the contract"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:375
msgid "The build-plugin interface (``PLUGIN_API_VERSION``) is 1.0 and uses protocol ``guidedog-build-plugin`` 1.0. The publisher interface (``API_VERSION``) is 1.1 and keeps ``guidedog-publisher`` protocol 1.0. These are separate contracts. The wire format is stable, so registered programs from earlier releases keep working. Product versions are separate from interface versions. New hooks arrive as trait methods with \"absent\" defaults, and host-made inputs are ``#[non_exhaustive]``, so a new field does not break a plugin. Contributed commands have their own command protocol (1.0), announced in optional handshake fields: programs without commands are unchanged."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:384
msgid "Register each rebuilt program deliberately, because its checksum changes. ``guidedog plugin inspect ID`` shows its kind and declared hooks. The public catalog describes plugins. It does not download, install or run them. Custom markup readers, renderers, directives and roles are not part of this interface. They use the ``guidedoc::Registry`` API in a program that embeds the engine."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:390
msgid "12. Check a plugin before registering it"
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:393
msgid "``guidedog plugin check PATH --project=DIR --config=FILE`` exercises a build plugin without changing the registry. Registration runs the same checks automatically: two fresh loads, repeated source and HTML results, generated asset validation and cleanup. ``--timeout=SECONDS`` defaults to 60 per process request. Supply a real configuration when the plugin needs one. Relative configuration paths resolve from ``--project``, whose default is the current directory."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:400
msgid "Passing checks shows that the plugin works with Guidedog. It is not a proof of safety. Guidedog keeps checking every runtime response and runs installed plugins in separate processes. A crash, bounds failure or stalled process becomes a diagnostic and cannot publish an incomplete generation. Linked plugins remain trusted code."
msgstr ""

#: ../../plugins.rst:405
msgid "Generic checks never invoke a publisher. Qualify publishers with their own tests and an explicitly configured dry run. The protocol and the SDK's design are recorded in :doc:`internals/plugins`."
msgstr ""
